From 9aa475502b4f1499fdf1b81fab442d38386f4527 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 02:49:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assets: seekable block compression (MBCZ) with a
 decompressing provider

Compresses the archives on the wire without changing a byte the guest sees.

lib/compressed-provider.js
  * MBCZ container: 32-byte header (magic/version/codec/blockSize/blockCount/
    originalSize/indexOffset/headerCRC32), independently deflated blocks, then a
    16-byte-per-block index (compressedOffset, compressedLength, originalLength,
    flags). Any read costs only the blocks it overlaps.
  * A block that does not compress is STORED and flagged, so an already-packed
    asset cannot grow.
  * Presents the ORIGINAL size, so ChunkCache, the VFS, preloadRanges and the
    guest all see an uncompressed file. Refuses to mount a container whose
    header CRC, block count, offsets or total length disagree -- and refuses a
    block whose inflated length differs from the index, rather than serving
    shifted bytes.

tools/compress-assets.js
  * Packs, and --verify re-inflates every block through the same parser and
    compares the reassembled file with the source.

host.js / lib/app-files.js / index.html
  * compressed:true plus compressedSize mounts the container through the
    provider inside the asset cache. lib/app-files.js passes such an entry
    through untouched: its size is the DECOMPRESSED length, so every size rule
    there (required whole-file preload, httpRange) would be answering about the
    wrong number.
  * Registered lib/compressed-provider.js in the page's script list.

test/test-compressed-provider.js
  * Real packer + real provider through the real ChunkCache: sequential,
    block-boundary, unaligned, past-EOF and sync reads all byte-exact; stored
    blocks asserted; four corruptions and an inflated-length mismatch refused.
    Verified to fail against the packer bug it was written for (a positioned
    header write followed by unpositioned payload writes overwrote the header).

Measured on this project's data shape: block size barely matters (64KB and 1MB
both land within ~1% of whole-file gzip on mixed assets), so 256KB is chosen for
1MB-read locality, not for ratio.
